[31m-# debug[m
[31m-[![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/visionmedia/debug.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/visionmedia/debug)  [![Coverage Status](https://coveralls.io/repos/github/visionmedia/debug/badge.svg?branch=master)](https://coveralls.io/github/visionmedia/debug?branch=master)  [![Slack](https://visionmedia-community-slackin.now.sh/badge.svg)](https://visionmedia-community-slackin.now.sh/) [![OpenCollective](https://opencollective.com/debug/backers/badge.svg)](#backers) [m
[31m-[![OpenCollective](https://opencollective.com/debug/sponsors/badge.svg)](#sponsors)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-[m
[31m-[m
[31m-A tiny node.js debugging utility modelled after node core's debugging technique.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-**Discussion around the V3 API is under way [here](https://github.com/visionmedia/debug/issues/370)**[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Installation[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```bash[m
[31m-$ npm install debug[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Usage[m
[31m-[m
[31m-`debug` exposes a function; simply pass this function the name of your module, and it will return a decorated version of `console.error` for you to pass debug statements to. This will allow you to toggle the debug output for different parts of your module as well as the module as a whole.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Example _app.js_:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-var debug = require('debug')('http')[m
[31m-  , http = require('http')[m
[31m-  , name = 'My App';[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// fake app[m
[31m-[m
[31m-debug('booting %s', name);[m
[31m-[m
[31m-http.createServer(function(req, res){[m
[31m-  debug(req.method + ' ' + req.url);[m
[31m-  res.end('hello\n');[m
[31m-}).listen(3000, function(){[m
[31m-  debug('listening');[m
[31m-});[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// fake worker of some kind[m
[31m-[m
[31m-require('./worker');[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Example _worker.js_:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-var debug = require('debug')('worker');[m
[31m-[m
[31m-setInterval(function(){[m
[31m-  debug('doing some work');[m
[31m-}, 1000);[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m- The __DEBUG__ environment variable is then used to enable these based on space or comma-delimited names. Here are some examples:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  ![debug http and worker](http://f.cl.ly/items/18471z1H402O24072r1J/Screenshot.png)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  ![debug worker](http://f.cl.ly/items/1X413v1a3M0d3C2c1E0i/Screenshot.png)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-#### Windows note[m
[31m-[m
[31m- On Windows the environment variable is set using the `set` command.[m
[31m-[m
[31m- ```cmd[m
[31m- set DEBUG=*,-not_this[m
[31m- ```[m
[31m-[m
[31m- Note that PowerShell uses different syntax to set environment variables.[m
[31m-[m
[31m- ```cmd[m
[31m- $env:DEBUG = "*,-not_this"[m
[31m-  ```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Then, run the program to be debugged as usual.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Millisecond diff[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  When actively developing an application it can be useful to see when the time spent between one `debug()` call and the next. Suppose for example you invoke `debug()` before requesting a resource, and after as well, the "+NNNms" will show you how much time was spent between calls.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  ![](http://f.cl.ly/items/2i3h1d3t121M2Z1A3Q0N/Screenshot.png)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  When stdout is not a TTY, `Date#toUTCString()` is used, making it more useful for logging the debug information as shown below:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  ![](http://f.cl.ly/items/112H3i0e0o0P0a2Q2r11/Screenshot.png)[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Conventions[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  If you're using this in one or more of your libraries, you _should_ use the name of your library so that developers may toggle debugging as desired without guessing names. If you have more than one debuggers you _should_ prefix them with your library name and use ":" to separate features. For example "bodyParser" from Connect would then be "connect:bodyParser".[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Wildcards[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  The `*` character may be used as a wildcard. Suppose for example your library has debuggers named "connect:bodyParser", "connect:compress", "connect:session", instead of listing all three with `DEBUG=connect:bodyParser,connect:compress,connect:session`, you may simply do `DEBUG=connect:*`, or to run everything using this module simply use `DEBUG=*`.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  You can also exclude specific debuggers by prefixing them with a "-" character.  For example, `DEBUG=*,-connect:*` would include all debuggers except those starting with "connect:".[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Environment Variables[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  When running through Node.js, you can set a few environment variables that will[m
[31m-  change the behavior of the debug logging:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| Name      | Purpose                                         |[m
[31m-|-----------|-------------------------------------------------|[m
[31m-| `DEBUG`   | Enables/disables specific debugging namespaces. |[m
[31m-| `DEBUG_COLORS`| Whether or not to use colors in the debug output. |[m
[31m-| `DEBUG_DEPTH` | Object inspection depth. |[m
[31m-| `DEBUG_SHOW_HIDDEN` | Shows hidden properties on inspected objects. |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  __Note:__ The environment variables beginning with `DEBUG_` end up being[m
[31m-  converted into an Options object that gets used with `%o`/`%O` formatters.[m
[31m-  See the Node.js documentation for[m
[31m-  [`util.inspect()`](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html#util_util_inspect_object_options)[m
[31m-  for the complete list.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Formatters[m
[31m-[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  Debug uses [printf-style](https://wikipedia.org/wiki/Printf_format_string) formatting. Below are the officially supported formatters:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| Formatter | Representation |[m
[31m-|-----------|----------------|[m
[31m-| `%O`      | Pretty-print an Object on multiple lines. |[m
[31m-| `%o`      | Pretty-print an Object all on a single line. |[m
[31m-| `%s`      | String. |[m
[31m-| `%d`      | Number (both integer and float). |[m
[31m-| `%j`      | JSON. Replaced with the string '[Circular]' if the argument contains circular references. |[m
[31m-| `%%`      | Single percent sign ('%'). This does not consume an argument. |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-### Custom formatters[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  You can add custom formatters by extending the `debug.formatters` object. For example, if you wanted to add support for rendering a Buffer as hex with `%h`, you could do something like:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-const createDebug = require('debug')[m
[31m-createDebug.formatters.h = (v) => {[m
[31m-  return v.toString('hex')[m
[31m-}[m
[31m-[m
[31m-// …elsewhere[m
[31m-const debug = createDebug('foo')[m
[31m-debug('this is hex: %h', new Buffer('hello world'))[m
[31m-//   foo this is hex: 68656c6c6f20776f726c6421 +0ms[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Browser support[m
[31m-  You can build a browser-ready script using [browserify](https://github.com/substack/node-browserify),[m
[31m-  or just use the [browserify-as-a-service](https://wzrd.in/) [build](https://wzrd.in/standalone/debug@latest),[m
[31m-  if you don't want to build it yourself.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-  Debug's enable state is currently persisted by `localStorage`.[m
[31m-  Consider the situation shown below where you have `worker:a` and `worker:b`,[m
[31m-  and wish to debug both. You can enable this using `localStorage.debug`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-localStorage.debug = 'worker:*'[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-And then refresh the page.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```js[m
[31m-a = debug('worker:a');[m
[31m-b = debug('worker:b');[m
